{"id":"u-d21c78eb-7ee6-42cb-8825-f86beb0916ed","src":"community","title":"Life:Workout Balance","author":"Anthony Caravaggi","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/C6SyyT-lifeworkout-balance","desc":"Built by an experienced lifter and coach with a demanding career and caregiving responsibilities, Life:Workout Balance is designed for the reality of managing a full life without letting your physical health slide. Training at this stage isn't about ego-lifting, chasing personal records, or living in the gym. It's about carving out time where you can to preserve muscle and build reliable strength and conditioning that carries over to your day-to-day.\n\nThe core philosophy focusses on high-density execution, combining strategic and compound movements with supersetted accessory blocks in a strict 65-to-70-minute window. It respects your recovery limits, recognizing that systemic stress from work and family directly impacts physical capacity. For nutrition and recovery, hit a reliable protein anchor, fuel your days sensibly, and eliminate the guilt of non-linear weeks. \n\nPay attention to the notes on some of the exercises - they contain important guidance, such as a 2-second pause on the hack squats.\n\nWeeks 1-4: Lock in technical execution, adapt to the density of the 65–70 minute time cap. Do not chase heavier weights. If you hit the lower end of your rep range with clean form and controlled RPE (7–8), maintain that exact load.\n\nWeeks 5-8: Drive progressive overload primarily through reps before load. Once you reach the top of the prescribed rep range across your working sets with room to spare, introduce a small weight increment (2.5kg for upper body, 5kg for lower body) in the following session.\n\nWeeks 9-12: Push working weights with high focus while respecting form and tempo rules. Keep top working sets honest by stopping 2 reps short of absolute failure. If life stress spikes and fatigue accumulates, pull back your back-off weights by roughly 10% to preserve movement quality rather than grinding through compromised reps.\n\nDeload week (week 13): Take a mandatory recovery week by cutting all working sets in half and reducing weights by 30–40%. This allows tissues and the central nervous system to fully recover before starting a new block.","level":["Intermediate","Advanced"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength","Athletics"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":70,"joins":0,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-26T04:07:12.861Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Upper/hinge","Posterior/capacity","Day 3"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"ss":[{"n":"Bird Dog","sets":[{"r":8,"x":2}]},{"n":"Cat Cow Stretch","sets":[{"r":8,"x":2}]},{"n":"Face Pull","sets":[{"r":15,"rpe":4,"x":2}]},{"n":"Reverse Lunge (Bodyweight)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":2}]}]},{"n":"Bench Press (Paused)","sets":[{"r":10,"pct":15},{"r":5,"pct":50},{"r":3,"pct":75},{"r":1,"pct":90}],"note":"1-sec pause on top set"},{"n":"Bench Press (Paused)","sets":[{"r":6,"rpe":8}]},{"n":"Bench Press (Paused)","sets":[{"r":10,"pct":70,"x":2}]},{"n":"Romanian Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"pct":50},{"r":6,"pct":70},{"r":10,"pct":80,"x":3}],"note":"Controlled tempo"},{"ss":[{"n":"Incline Bench Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":10,"rpe":7,"x":3}],"note":"Deep stretch at bottom"},{"n":"Chest Supported Row (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":10,"rpe":7,"x":3}],"note":"Squeeze shoulder blades at top"}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Dip (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":10,"rpe":7,"x":3}],"note":"Upright torso"},{"n":"Tricep Pushdown (Cable)","sets":[{"r":10,"rpe":7,"x":3}],"note":"Lock elbows in"}]},{"n":"Ab Wheel","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":7,"x":3}]},{"n":"Treadmill","sets":[{"sec":600}]}]},{"ex":[{"ss":[{"n":"Glute Bridge (Bodyweight)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":2}]},{"n":"Hip Openers","sets":[{"r":8,"x":2}]},{"n":"Spider-Man Lunge","sets":[{"r":8,"x":2}]}]},{"n":"Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":40},{"r":3,"pct":60},{"r":2,"pct":75},{"r":1,"pct":90}],"note":"Strict stop every rep. No bouncing."},{"n":"Deadlift (Barbell)","se